✅ What are Class Loaders?
Class loaders dynamically load .class bytecode into the JVM at runtime.
Every class is identified by: fully qualified name + its ClassLoader
(same name, different loaders → different Class objects).


🔄 Types of Class Loaders

1) Bootstrap ClassLoader
- Loads core JDK classes: java.lang.*, java.util.*, …
- Written in native code (C/C++), part of JVM
- Parent of all other loaders (its parent is null)
- getClassLoader() for String.class → null  (bootstrap)

Where it loads from:
  Old story: jre/lib
  Modern (Java 9+): modular runtime image / JDK modules


2) Extension → Platform ClassLoader (Java 9+)
- Old (pre-9): Extension ClassLoader → lib/ext or java.ext.dirs
- Java 9+: Platform ClassLoader → platform modules
  (e.g. java.sql, java.desktop, …)
- Child of Bootstrap


3) System / Application ClassLoader
- Loads your app classes from classpath:
  -cp, CLASSPATH, or application module path
- Child of Platform ClassLoader
- Most application classes load here
- ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader() returns this


4) Custom ClassLoader
- Extend java.lang.ClassLoader (often override findClass)
- Use cases:
  - load from encrypted / remote / DB sources
  - isolate apps (Tomcat webapps, OSGi)
  - hot reload (Spring Boot DevTools)


🔁 Hierarchy + Parent Delegation Model

        Bootstrap          (parent = null)
            ↑
   Extension / Platform
            ↑
   System (Application)
            ↑
      Custom (if any)

How loading works (parent first):
1) ClassLoader receives request for class X
2) Delegate to parent first
3) Parent delegates upward …
4) If Bootstrap/parent finds X → use that
5) Only if parents fail → current loader tries to find/load X itself

Why parent delegation?
- Security / consistency: app cannot “replace” java.lang.String with a fake one
- Avoids duplicate loading of core classes


⚡ Interview extras

Phases (when a class is first actively used):
  Loading → Linking (verify, prepare, resolve) → Initialization (statics)

Breaking / bypassing delegation (real world):
  Some containers load webapp classes first (child-first) for isolation —
  opposite of default parent-first. Know that default Java model is parent delegation.

Visibility:
  Child can see classes loaded by parent
  Parent generally cannot see classes loaded by child


💬 Quick Q&A

Q: Which loader loads your Main class?
A: Application (System) ClassLoader (usually).

Q: Why is String’s class loader null?
A: Loaded by Bootstrap → represented as null in Java APIs.

Q: Can two loaders load the same class name?
A: Yes → two different runtime Class types (can cause ClassCastException across boundaries).


🧠 Interview one-liner
“Java uses a hierarchy of class loaders with parent delegation: Bootstrap → Platform → Application → custom;
a loader asks its parent first so core classes stay safe and unique.”
